# Channel strategy (future plans)

Two channels may exist later. Do not merge them into one generic “AI aesthetic/ASMR” account. Keep one promise per channel.

## Channel A — After Hours Japan (primary / ship first)

- Empty night places, locked 9:16, no people/faces, soft ambient hum/drip/fridge.
- Brand = after-hours mood (Japan-first). Use `#asmr #aesthetic #liminal` in captions only — not as the channel identity.
- Series pattern: short title card (“Level B2.”) + 2–3 visual beats.
- Expand via new rooms/angles/weather under the same vibe; do not go fully generic.

### Early analytics (mid-Sep 2026) — bias future batches

Performance so far tracks **viewer stop-scroll + retention**, not algorithm trivia. Early winners:

- **Hook (stayed to watch):** strong first-frame sensory hit — e.g. Sento ~29% stayed-to-watch.
- **Retention (avg % viewed):** comfort + rain + food holds longest — e.g. Late Night Ramen ~83% APV, solid browse CTR.
- **Theme pull:** late-night food / cozy rain / warm interiors beat pure liminal voids (ferry, laundry-type) for curiosity.

When generating again (after the analytics gate):

1. First **0.5–1s** = unmistakable audio + motion (sizzle, rain hit, neon flicker, steam). ECU openers stay non-negotiable.
2. Prefer **comfort/food/wet-street** subjects over abstract empty places unless the ECU hook is unmistakable.
3. Titles = sensory scene (“Late Night Ramen in the Rain”), not place labels.
4. Optional: sub-15s seamless loops for rewatch >100% APV.
5. Give brand-new uploads time across viewing hours before judging them.

## Channel B — Speculative science / megastructures (second channel or later pivot)

- One mind-bending question per short with a concrete payoff (e.g. “How would a Dyson swarm dump heat?”).
- 2–3 locked or slow visuals; one shared art bible every episode (lighting/color/style locked).
- Short VO; one scientific principle; one lingering end image.
- No people — same quality rule as Channel A. Visualize the unfilmable; avoid “pretty CGI + vague voiceover.”

## Optional lane — Folklore / mythology micro-docs

- Only if preferring eerie vibe over smart explainers.
- One creature/rite, one culture, one eerie fact; atmosphere > gore.
- Silhouette / place / object only — no creature-face continuity across shots.
- Prefer obscure regional myths; same episode structure every time.

## Explicitly defer

- Cinematic history battles: crowded + AI consistency (faces, armor, era, geography) is too fragile for a first/second channel with current tools.

## Production defaults (both channels)

- Prefer no people / no faces / silhouettes when possible.
- Same look every post beats niche-hopping.
- One channel at a time until posting cadence is real.

## Active phase (Sep 2026) — post inventory, then decide

- **Generation paused.** Network volume torn down until we deliberately restart renders.
- Post only **QC-passed** ready hooks (`final_videos/ready/*_hook.mp4`). Skip broken shots (e.g. s2_ep03 bookstore last-slide gibberish text) unless trimmed/fixed.
- Follow `POSTING_SCHEDULE.md` (1 Short/day, ~21:00 IST).
- Mid-backlog early signal (ramen/sento) is **direction only** (~150–190 views) — finish the queue, then wait.
- After the good backlog is live (~23 Sep+), **stop and read analytics** for ~7–14 days (views, avg % viewed / engaged views, stayed-to-watch, subs).
  - If retention/reach look promising → restore volume and continue After Hours Japan, biased by the early-analytics notes above.
  - If not → explore other niches (Channel B speculative science is first candidate; don’t go generic ASMR).
- Do not open a second channel or burn GPU until that analytics gate is done.
